1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:02,980
This man sold the Eiffel Tower.

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:04,900
There was just one problem.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:06,570
He didn't own it.

4
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,960
Paris, 1925.

5
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:11,750
Victor Lustig spotted a newspaper story

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,240
about the tower's expensive maintenance.

7
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,420
So he forged government letters

8
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:17,940
and summoned scrap-metal dealers

9
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:19,430
to a luxury hotel.

10
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:20,600
His announcement?

11
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:23,660
Paris was secretly dismantling the tower.

12
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,200
And its seven thousand tons

13
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:26,900
of iron were up for sale.

14
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:29,430
The dealers were told to keep quiet.

15
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:31,590
One of them, André Poisson,

16
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,020
wanted the deal—but had doubts.

17
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:35,850
So Lustig hinted that a little

18
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:37,950
private payment could secure it.

19
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:39,620
Poisson paid.

20
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,290
Lustig took the money and disappeared.

21
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,750
Only then did Poisson realize

22
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:46,600
the documents were fake.

23
00:00:46,970 --> 00:00:47,960
He was so embarrassed,

24
00:00:47,970 --> 00:00:49,920
he reportedly stayed silent.

25
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:51,640
And the strangest part?

26
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:54,570
Lustig tried the same trick again.

27
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:56,560
This time, he failed.

28
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,560
The Eiffel Tower was never his to sell.

